    I've got a Z SL-5500 and it runs NetHack just fine and dandy.  Nice 
port ... I'm currently using 3.4.0, but since I see there's a newer one 
out I may have to give it a try.
    The standard 5500 Z has about 22MB (or so) of free storage (of 32) 
by default.  That's plenty for NetHack *and* some actual data!  (grin)
    BUT I ran out of main storage (that pesky swap file takes a good 
chunk, and support libs take another), and NetHack doesn't appear to 
like running from the SD.  There are a *lot* of things that claim they 
will only work from internal storage, but when one installs them there, 
copies the directory/ies to SD, then soft-links back to the original 
location, they actually run quite nicely.  MySQL, for example, has been 
running on my Z for days after stuffing it on the SD this way.  Try 
*that* on your PalmOS.
    BUT NetHack won't play nice with this method, so I'm currently sans 
dungeon crawl until I can figure out what's up with it, as I'm not 
willing to sacrafice 4MB of my precious remaining internal storage (or 
offload a lib file to slower-loading flash memory) for something that 
should, in my estimation, run just fine from SD.  Everything *else* does.
   (shrug)
    Overall, if you're looking for one awesome piece of hardware, sans 
cell capability, that with a swap file will run at least eight 
applications simultaneously, and the *MOST* customizable OS of any 
handheld device in the world, a Z isn't a bad choice.  It does totally 
lack any sort of decent sound capability, but that'll be fixed on the 
SL-6000 that the rumor mill has set for January '04 release.  (expectant 
drool)
    I'm confident the NetHack sitch will be resolved.  Like just about 
any Linux installation, with perserverance comes success...

